<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Greece</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Greece is a region in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> on the Balkan peninsula and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> islands, the home of ancient Greek civilisation. It was the target of Achaemenid invasions, came under Macedonian dominance, and was contested by Hellenistic powers including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>. In later periods it was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, and in the twentieth century it was invaded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/greece.jpg" alt="Greece" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Greece.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…a federation of Peloponnesian cities led by Aratus and Philopoemen, was the most successful federal state of ancient <b>Greece</b>, uniting the whole Peloponnese in 191 BCE before Rome crushed it in 146 BCE; through Polybius it became a model for modern federal thinkers, including the authors of The…</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>From the Persian perspective, however, the failure to subjugate <b>Greece</b> was a peripheral setback that left the empire's Asian and African heartland untouched.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Macedonian society was less urban than that of southern <b>Greece</b>, though old and new cities flourished: Pella was the royal seat, Thessalonica and Cassandreia were major ports, and Demetrias, founded by Demetrius Poliorcetes around 293 BCE on the Gulf of Pagasae, became a royal residence and fortress.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Unlike many Mycenaean centres it was not destroyed around 1200 BCE, and in the early Iron Age it remained one of the largest communities in <b>Greece</b>, known for its Protogeometric and Geometric pottery from the cemetery of the Kerameikos.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Boeotia, the fertile plain of central <b>Greece</b> between Attica and Phocis, held a dozen or more independent cities, of which Thebes was the largest, followed by Orchomenus, Thespiae, Tanagra and Plataea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The final century and a half was one of progressive Ottoman encirclement: the Ottomans established themselves in Europe at Gallipoli in 1354, moved their capital to Adrianople (Edirne), and reduced the empire to the city itself and scattered remnants in <b>Greece</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
